{:edb/db-before-t 1981 :edb/db-after-t 1982 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535294 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:27:16.215Z" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124344 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/859a831dcc11fa6ffe430cc913fa46283d3231816429d3fea98219fe3417b746.png)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\n- $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124344 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n3. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124344 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}$. So, it's a steady-state vector of $P$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124344 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/a172ba62aed6c25fc1b40fdf70b1ae88a7e33d2f3341696783b96defd927b6be.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124345 1100 "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124345 1100 "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase.\n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124345 1145 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P$, and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\nPq = q⟹(P - I)q = 0\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_{1} = 8x_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x_{2} + x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\n9x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{2} &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}\n$$\n\nof the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600\n$$" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124345 1145 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/1935c4960a48e2b446c2610b9edc2a160a5f45462494c72a90f139ea2d13cc34.png)\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/29b5a209bb27b24f165ee418326763d8fb24f83f2b3a767052c381aa8cd0e01c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/0e8f620c3392dbb77b1353184e38eaf48b526aa82c502772a5582bd20c7f5aea.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/2ed7b0fc7296b2b651909c7c15c73e253fc8a9f95fb037072228d5de6f838894.png)\n\nSo, ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}$ of the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600.\n$$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124346 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/093de94431946cb83e7cc0523405989b7a23cc8d9677bdecdfba7aaf207fd62b.png)\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." 13194139535294 true] [17592186124346 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." 13194139535294 false] [17592186124346 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPq &= q \\\\\nPq - q &= 0 \\\\\nPq - Iq &= 0 \\\\\n(P - I)q &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation\n\n$$\n(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=20R_{1} \\\\ R_{2}:=10R_{2} \\\\ R_{3}:=20R_{3} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}↔R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 9R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} - 7R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\n2x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124346 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/5ed2f76d9b2857134a5b2aa5781711f9625cabb46a9ca3c3d54aa31c32bb4dbe.png)\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=20{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=20{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/9ae5533dc825d2d3d08ca7a54c50578e21dda8fad7988b9043baaad2a69368b7.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061943/assets/fd7775b5d982d0bc51e80417a70b67dbff0176afcc5ff9aec613947076201386.png)" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124536 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342224 13194139535294 true] [17592186124536 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342231 13194139535294 true] [17592186124536 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342238 13194139535294 true] [17592186124537 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342245 13194139535294 true] [17592186124537 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342252 13194139535294 true] [17592186124537 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342259 13194139535294 true] [17592186124537 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342280 13194139535294 true] [17592186124538 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342266 13194139535294 true] [17592186124538 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342273 13194139535294 true] [17592186124538 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342287 13194139535294 true] [17592186124538 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342294 13194139535294 true] [17592186124538 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342301 13194139535294 true] [17592186124688 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535294 true] [17592186124688 1100 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124688 1100 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124688 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124689 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535294 true] [17592186124689 1100 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124689 1100 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124689 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124690 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535294 true] [17592186124690 1100 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186124690 1100 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$, what is the value of $a - b$?" 13194139535294 false] [17592186124690 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342225 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1089 "q-135306" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1100 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1113 #uuid "224b3ef2-275a-5b9b-8943-2770e7619fc4" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342224 1145 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342226 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342227 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342228 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342229 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342230 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1041 "selection" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342227 13194139535294 true] [17592186342225 1110 #uuid "83cc77b2-be86-5143-9d4f-fe8f63aa2909" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342226 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 1319413953529