 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 26 & -26 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 1.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -26 & 26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{5}$ of the customers will consume rice $B.$ Therefore, the number of people who will eventually consume rice $B$ will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 200\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342289 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342290 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342291 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342292 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342293 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1041 "selection" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342293 13194139535294 true] [17592186342288 1110 #uuid "803dbf47-bd15-593e-a307-ee40b421c90b" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342289 1034 "52\\,500" 13194139535294 true] [1759218634228