s, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{7}$ of the people will prefer baseball. Therefore, the number of people who will prefer baseball is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 1\\,260=360\\,\\text{people}.\n$$" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342303 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342304 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342305 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342306 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342307 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1041 "selection" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342307 13194139535294 true] [17592186342302 1110 #uuid "94bb32e2-c8af-5d16-8c0a-d4734611d04c" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342303 1034 "180" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535294 true] [17592186342303 1111 #uuid "07cd4c8d-a940-5ddd-b100-55c57b972ce9" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342304 1034 "540" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535294 true] [17592186342304 1111 #uuid "988496d8-e783-5faa-8afc-00a7da709fda" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342305 1034 "630" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535294 true] [17592186342305 1111 #uuid "832636af-5e32-5e8e-a70f-0899d4733c5b" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342306 1034 "720" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535294 true] [17592186342306 1111 #uuid "c51165d8-9d04-581d-b131-d4615e6ea7b3" 13194139535294 true] [17592186342307 1034 "360" 13194139535294 true] [17592